300: The following definition records the assumptions on the parameters used in the preceding considerations.
301: \begin{definition}
302: Call an integer tuple $\varphi=(n,r,i,j,a,b,N)$ \emph{valid} if $n,r,i,j,a,b,N\geq 1$ with
303: \[r<n,\quad a+j\leq r,\quad a+i\leq n-r,\quad b\leq i,j,\quad\mbox{and}\quad N\leq ab. \]
304: \end{definition}

326: \begin{lemma}
327: \label{lem:implication-of-syzygy}
328: Suppose for some valid $\varphi=(n,r,i,j,a,b,N)$ there is a homogeneous syzygy of degree $N$ in $\overline{\Lambda}$ of the form
329: \[\sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\|\lambda|=N}}\overline{s}_{(i^j,\lambda)}f_{\lambda}=\sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\|\lambda|<N }}f_{\lambda}\overline{s}_{(i^j,\lambda)}\]
330: with the left-hand side nonzero.
331: Then the decomposable equations
332: \begin{equation}
333: \label{eqn:decomposable}
334: \biggr\{ \sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\|\lambda|<N }}\sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r\\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}} c_{(i^j,\lambda),\theta}^{\nu} \, A_{\lambda,\theta}=\chi(\nu)\,\biggr| \,\nu\in \decomp_{\varphi} \biggr\}
335: \end{equation}
336: are consistent, where $\chi(\nu)\in\mathbb{Z}$ is defined for $\nu\in \decomp_{\varphi}$ by
337: \[\chi(\nu)=\begin{cases}
338: f_{\nu_B} &\mbox{ if $\nu$ is tall,}\\
339: 0 &\mbox{ if $\nu$ is wide.}
340: \end{cases} \]
341: \end{lemma}

342: \begin{proof}
343: The above considerations show that the lemma is true if we replace (\ref{eqn:decomposable}) with the superset (\ref{eqn:eqs}). Clearly, removing equations from a consistent linear system yields a consistent linear system.
344: \end{proof}
